The full test suite now passes: `npm test` ran 162 suites with 0 failures. The last run had 5 failing suites.

The build-loop error deduplication itself was already in place from commit `d17891ce`. That covers signature normalization and hashing, consecutive-repeat detection, the escalation ladder, the `error_repeat_restart` restart, and the `loop.error_dedup_*` config flags. Its tests in `sw-loop-test.sh` all passed in the failing run too. Each of the 5 failing suites passed when I ran it on its own, so this iteration went to finding why they failed in the full run.

**Fixed (commit `fde4342c`):**
- **`sw-self-optimize.sh` (a real bug):** the memory pruning and strengthening cutoffs used `date -u -r <epoch>`, which only works on macOS. On Linux that call fails and quietly falls back to the current time. A pattern written in the same second was then kept or deleted depending on whether the clock had ticked over. It now uses the existing cross-platform `epoch_to_iso` helper from `compat.sh`. Before the fix the suite failed 2 of 16 runs; after it, 0 of 10.
- **`sw-daemon-test.sh` and `sw-loop-test.sh` (test-only):** 37 checks piped into `grep -q` under `pipefail`. When `grep -q` stops reading early, the command feeding it can be killed, and a match gets reported as a miss under heavy load. That accounts for the daemon failures and the "Loop stuckness detection" failure. I changed these to forms that don't stop early. All three suites pass, and my change adds no shellcheck warnings.

**Not fixed — both passed in this full run:**
- **`sw-e2e-integration-test`** hit a lock: "A pipeline is already in progress". That is most likely this loop's own pipeline holding it, not a code fault.
- **`sw-intent-analysis-test`** failed once in the full run and passed 4 of 4 times on its own. I didn't find the cause, so it may still fail occasionally.

The previous audit's feedback was an error in the audit tool's JSON schema, not a finding about the code, so there was nothing in it to fix.

LOOP_COMPLETE
